1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,432
Hi, everyone, and thank you for joining.

2
00:00:02,976 --> 00:00:08,201
My name is Denis Galkin, and I am
Android engineer at Robofinance.

3
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:13,941
Today, I wanna talk about something
every mobile team has, but very few

4
00:00:13,942 --> 00:00:16,871
teams design on purpose, internal tools.

5
00:00:17,430 --> 00:00:21,384
Things like a screen to send a test
push, a switch to change the date

6
00:00:21,397 --> 00:00:27,179
inside the app, or a menu to change the
server environment At this conference,

7
00:00:27,203 --> 00:00:32,762
we talk a lot about platforms like
CI/CD, Kubernetes, developer portals.

8
00:00:32,990 --> 00:00:37,704
My talk is about a much smaller platform,
one that lives inside a mobile app.

9
00:00:38,131 --> 00:00:41,838
We call it Dev Mode
I'll cover three things.

10
00:00:42,085 --> 00:00:43,244
First, the problem.

11
00:00:43,589 --> 00:00:46,970
What happens to internal tools
when nobody designed them?

12
00:00:47,183 --> 00:00:48,731
Second, the solution.

13
00:00:49,188 --> 00:00:53,388
How we build Dev Mode as a plugin
framework and how it works now inside.

14
00:00:54,230 --> 00:00:56,224
And third, the lessons.

15
00:00:56,495 --> 00:01:00,751
Why it was adopted without any
migration or pressure, and, what

16
00:01:00,755 --> 00:01:02,143
I would do differently today.

17
00:01:02,611 --> 00:01:06,877
The examples are from Android, but
the ideas work for any platform

18
00:01:06,879 --> 00:01:09,699
team Let's start with the problem.

19
00:01:10,099 --> 00:01:13,786
Almost every screen in a mobile
app shows data from a server.

20
00:01:14,005 --> 00:01:18,774
To test a screen properly, you often
need to change something, the date, a

21
00:01:18,775 --> 00:01:21,313
server response, or a push notification.

22
00:01:21,966 --> 00:01:25,534
So developers built a small
tools for that, and that's fine.

23
00:01:25,761 --> 00:01:27,477
but the problem starts later

24
00:01:28,477 --> 00:01:32,032
One developer adds a hidden
button on the profile screen.

25
00:01:32,101 --> 00:01:35,272
Another one adds a debug
menu in the settings.

26
00:01:35,897 --> 00:01:38,917
A third one adds a secret
gesture somewhere else.

27
00:01:39,312 --> 00:01:44,479
Each tool has its own small UI, its own
navigation, and its own way to open it.

28
00:01:44,688 --> 00:01:45,920
Nobody planned this.

29
00:01:46,335 --> 00:01:47,445
It just grows.

30
00:01:47,655 --> 00:01:52,652
After a while, you have many tools, but
no system, and everyone pays for it.

31
00:01:54,468 --> 00:01:57,824
Developers pay because every
new tool starts from zero.

32
00:01:57,914 --> 00:01:58,946
Where do I put it?

33
00:01:59,127 --> 00:02:00,131
How do I open it?

34
00:02:00,291 --> 00:02:01,359
How do I go back?

35
00:02:01,572 --> 00:02:04,842
It's the same infrastructure
work again and again.

36
00:02:05,465 --> 00:02:09,616
QA engineers pay because they need
these tools every day, but they have

37
00:02:09,616 --> 00:02:14,550
to remember where each one lives or
ask a developer, and the developer

38
00:02:14,550 --> 00:02:16,469
stops their work to answer it.

39
00:02:16,950 --> 00:02:21,350
The company pays too because each
app ends up with its own debug menu.

40
00:02:21,525 --> 00:02:27,827
Different look, different logic, nothing
shared If this sounds familiar, it's the

41
00:02:27,827 --> 00:02:29,817
classic platform engineering problem.

42
00:02:30,043 --> 00:02:33,202
Teams build the same thing
many times in different ways,

43
00:02:33,250 --> 00:02:34,845
and nobody owns the result.

44
00:02:35,327 --> 00:02:37,006
Here, it just happens inside OneApp.

45
00:02:38,519 --> 00:02:40,302
So what did we do?

46
00:02:40,628 --> 00:02:44,674
We built Dev Mode as a plugin
framework, and we did it early, before

47
00:02:44,685 --> 00:02:46,586
the tool had a chance to spread.

48
00:02:47,053 --> 00:02:49,020
I'll come back to why that matters

49
00:02:50,020 --> 00:02:51,837
The main idea is simple.

50
00:02:52,146 --> 00:02:55,915
We split Dev Mode into two parts,
the shell and the content.

51
00:02:56,155 --> 00:03:01,530
The framework owns the shell, how you open
Dev Mode, the Dev Mode screen itself, the list

52
00:03:01,530 --> 00:03:03,758
of tools, and the navigation between them.

53
00:03:03,927 --> 00:03:08,175
The app owns the content, which
tools exist, what they're called,

54
00:03:08,281 --> 00:03:11,395
what their screens look like,
and what they actually do.

55
00:03:11,594 --> 00:03:15,265
That means their view model,
their logic, and their settings.

56
00:03:17,187 --> 00:03:20,447
The framework doesn't
know what mock date means.

57
00:03:20,703 --> 00:03:23,228
It doesn't know what a test push is.

58
00:03:23,515 --> 00:03:28,601
For the framework, every tool is just
a plugin, a name and a screen to open.

59
00:03:29,582 --> 00:03:32,157
If you know Backstage,
this will feel familiar.

60
00:03:32,429 --> 00:03:35,975
A small core and everything
useful lives in plugins.

61
00:03:36,193 --> 00:03:41,238
It's the same idea, but at the
scale of one mobile app Why

62
00:03:41,241 --> 00:03:43,026
does the split matter so much?

63
00:03:43,177 --> 00:03:46,092
Because the two parts
change at different speeds.

64
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:47,496
The shell is stable.

65
00:03:47,571 --> 00:03:50,102
Once it works, it almost never changes.

66
00:03:50,579 --> 00:03:55,202
The content changes all the time
because new features need new tools.

67
00:03:55,435 --> 00:03:58,637
If you mix the two, every
new tool touches shared code.

68
00:03:58,978 --> 00:04:03,131
If you separate them, the shared
code stays quiet, and all the

69
00:04:03,132 --> 00:04:05,124
changes happen in the plugins

70
00:04:06,124 --> 00:04:10,071
So the framework says, "Give me
your plugins, and I'll show them in

71
00:04:10,072 --> 00:04:12,438
one place in one consistent way."

72
00:04:12,549 --> 00:04:14,833
And the app says, "Here are my plugins."

73
00:04:15,202 --> 00:04:17,778
So let's see how this work in practice.

74
00:04:19,527 --> 00:04:22,135
Everything starts with
a way to open dev mode.

75
00:04:22,495 --> 00:04:23,807
We use a classic pattern.

76
00:04:23,819 --> 00:04:27,504
You tap a certain element several
times, and dev mode opens.

77
00:04:28,012 --> 00:04:29,566
In the app, this takes one line.

78
00:04:29,582 --> 00:04:33,504
For a regular Android view, we call
initDevModeListener called on it.

79
00:04:33,757 --> 00:04:38,063
For Jetpack Compose, there is a
modifier, modifier.DevModeClickable.

80
00:04:38,639 --> 00:04:39,892
That's all the app does.

81
00:04:39,988 --> 00:04:42,824
It only says, "This
element opens dev mode."

82
00:04:43,005 --> 00:04:48,139
It doesn't know anything about how
activation works inside Inside the

83
00:04:48,147 --> 00:04:52,257
framework, a small class called
Dev Mode Runner counts the taps.

84
00:04:52,478 --> 00:04:54,280
By default, you need five.

85
00:04:54,550 --> 00:04:57,789
Every tap increases the
counter and starts a timer.

86
00:04:57,961 --> 00:05:01,862
If you make five taps in time,
the framework opens its own

87
00:05:01,863 --> 00:05:04,203
screen called Dev Mode Activity.

88
00:05:04,568 --> 00:05:07,055
If you are too slow, the count resets.

89
00:05:07,398 --> 00:05:11,637
So the app doesn't write a
counter, a timer, a reset, or

90
00:05:11,656 --> 00:05:13,070
the code that opens Dev Mode.

91
00:05:13,454 --> 00:05:20,077
It's all in the framework It looks like a
small detail, but it shows the main rule.

92
00:05:20,483 --> 00:05:23,845
The app makes only the decisions
that belong to the app.

93
00:05:24,373 --> 00:05:27,021
Which element opens
DevMode is an app decision.

94
00:05:27,412 --> 00:05:29,084
How to count taps isn't.

95
00:05:30,889 --> 00:05:35,633
After five taps, we are in DevMode
activity, the main entry point of the

96
00:05:35,642 --> 00:05:40,594
framework, and here is the most important
design decision in the whole framework.

97
00:05:40,929 --> 00:05:44,917
DevMode activity doesn't know
anything about which plugins exist

98
00:05:45,916 --> 00:05:50,895
Instead, it depends on an interface
called IDevModePluginEntriesProvider.

99
00:05:51,279 --> 00:05:53,092
It has one method, entries.

100
00:05:53,351 --> 00:05:56,899
This method returns navigation
entries for Navigation 3,

101
00:05:57,065 --> 00:05:58,997
Google's new navigation library.

102
00:05:59,089 --> 00:06:03,050
Simply put, through this interface,
the app can add its own screens

103
00:06:03,310 --> 00:06:04,670
to the framework's navigation.

104
00:06:05,017 --> 00:06:07,120
So the framework defines the contract.

105
00:06:07,181 --> 00:06:10,742
If you wanna add new screens
to DevMode, register them here

106
00:06:11,742 --> 00:06:13,251
And the app implements it.

107
00:06:13,465 --> 00:06:18,897
In our app, the implementation is called
AppDevModePluginEntriesProvider, and this

108
00:06:18,898 --> 00:06:21,326
is where the real tools are registered.

109
00:06:21,437 --> 00:06:24,282
Today, I'll show two of them as examples.

110
00:06:24,469 --> 00:06:29,971
TestPushesDevPluginScreen is for test push
notifications, and MockDatePluginScreen

111
00:06:30,007 --> 00:06:32,335
is for changing the date inside the app.

112
00:06:34,037 --> 00:06:35,441
Let's look at MockDate.

113
00:06:35,636 --> 00:06:39,673
It has its own screen, its own
view model, and its own repository.

114
00:06:39,791 --> 00:06:41,512
All of that lives in the app.

115
00:06:41,751 --> 00:06:43,364
The framework never sees it.

116
00:06:43,501 --> 00:06:47,211
For the framework, MockDate is
just one more navigation entry.

117
00:06:49,201 --> 00:06:52,625
You can even see this boundary
in the project structure.

118
00:06:52,648 --> 00:06:57,822
The framework lives in its own package,
and the plugins live in the app's package,

119
00:06:58,157 --> 00:07:00,461
and this gives us reuse almost for free.

120
00:07:00,652 --> 00:07:04,420
If another app wants dev mode,
it doesn't take our plugins.

121
00:07:04,892 --> 00:07:08,726
It implements its own provider
with its own screens, and the

122
00:07:08,728 --> 00:07:10,640
framework stays exactly the same.

123
00:07:10,935 --> 00:07:15,650
In platform terms, this interface
is our API, the main contract

124
00:07:15,674 --> 00:07:17,704
between the framework and the app

125
00:07:18,704 --> 00:07:23,116
Now the screens are registered,
but the user also needs a menu,

126
00:07:23,286 --> 00:07:24,874
a list of tools to choose from.

127
00:07:25,050 --> 00:07:27,889
Inside dev mode, there is
a tab called Dev Plugins.

128
00:07:27,991 --> 00:07:29,567
This is Dev Plugins screen.

129
00:07:30,063 --> 00:07:33,766
The screen gets its data from a
view model, and the view model gets

130
00:07:33,783 --> 00:07:37,401
it from another small interface
called IDevPluginsProvider.

131
00:07:37,892 --> 00:07:41,313
It returns a list of dev
plugin data transfer objects.

132
00:07:41,693 --> 00:07:45,959
Each one is very simple: an index,
a title, and a screen to open.

133
00:07:46,136 --> 00:07:48,438
In other words, here is the menu item.

134
00:07:48,883 --> 00:07:49,889
This is its name.

135
00:07:50,129 --> 00:07:55,394
When you tap it, open the screen The
screen shows the list in a lazy column,

136
00:07:55,535 --> 00:07:57,352
which is a simple scrolling list.

137
00:07:57,466 --> 00:08:01,031
When you tap an item, the view
model calls the router route to

138
00:08:01,032 --> 00:08:02,983
screen with the plugin screen.

139
00:08:03,337 --> 00:08:04,840
The router is simple too.

140
00:08:05,018 --> 00:08:08,631
It takes a navigation key, a
nav key, and navigates to it.

141
00:08:08,742 --> 00:08:13,546
It doesn't know which screens exist
And this is the part I like most.

142
00:08:13,653 --> 00:08:16,009
There is no when statement
in the framework.

143
00:08:16,117 --> 00:08:18,125
That's Kotlin's version of switch.

144
00:08:18,222 --> 00:08:22,726
There is no code that says if the
user picked mock date, open mock date.

145
00:08:22,839 --> 00:08:25,547
If they pick test
pushes, open test pushes.

146
00:08:25,762 --> 00:08:27,742
The framework never makes that decision.

147
00:08:28,089 --> 00:08:33,955
It gets a key from the app and passes it
to navigation So here is the full path.

148
00:08:34,541 --> 00:08:36,875
A tap on a view or a compose element.

149
00:08:37,169 --> 00:08:39,249
Dev Mode runner counts the taps.

150
00:08:39,470 --> 00:08:41,313
Dev Mode activity opens.

151
00:08:41,489 --> 00:08:44,806
The dev plugins tab shows
the list from the provider.

152
00:08:45,311 --> 00:08:46,648
The user picks a tool.

153
00:08:47,168 --> 00:08:51,959
The router navigates by its key, and the
screen that opens belongs to the app.

154
00:08:52,307 --> 00:08:53,989
The framework builds the route.

155
00:08:54,545 --> 00:08:56,449
The app decides where it leads.

156
00:08:58,009 --> 00:09:00,933
What does this mean for a
developer who needs a new tool?

157
00:09:01,454 --> 00:09:04,143
Let's say we need a tool
to switch feature flags.

158
00:09:04,239 --> 00:09:07,614
The developer does four
things, all inside the app.

159
00:09:07,993 --> 00:09:11,079
One, create the screen
and its own view model.

160
00:09:11,779 --> 00:09:14,465
Two, describe a navigation
key for this screen.

161
00:09:14,913 --> 00:09:19,252
Three, register the screen in
AppDevModePluginEntriesProvider.

162
00:09:19,641 --> 00:09:22,163
Four, add an item to the plugin list.

163
00:09:22,573 --> 00:09:23,087
That's it.

164
00:09:23,465 --> 00:09:27,335
The new tool appears in Dev Mode
with the same look and the same

165
00:09:27,339 --> 00:09:29,068
navigation as every other tool.

166
00:09:30,279 --> 00:09:32,524
Now notice what's missing from this list.

167
00:09:32,837 --> 00:09:36,015
You don't change Dev Mode activity
or the plugin list screen.

168
00:09:36,183 --> 00:09:38,868
You don't add a new branch
to any navigation code.

169
00:09:39,513 --> 00:09:41,364
The framework isn't touched at all.

170
00:09:41,932 --> 00:09:46,640
The registration and the menu item
take a few minutes, and all the rest

171
00:09:46,683 --> 00:09:48,760
of the time goes into the tool itself.

172
00:09:49,155 --> 00:09:50,205
That's how it should be.

173
00:09:50,528 --> 00:09:54,094
The cost of a new tool should
be the cost of the tool, not the

174
00:09:54,097 --> 00:09:55,749
cost of the platform around it.

175
00:09:56,135 --> 00:10:01,379
The plugins or twenty, the framework
stays the same The name says Dev,

176
00:10:01,613 --> 00:10:04,253
but developers aren't the only users.

177
00:10:04,471 --> 00:10:09,661
Today, three groups use DevMode:
developers, QA engineers, and managers.

178
00:10:10,118 --> 00:10:13,128
For developers, it's a standard
place for any debug tool.

179
00:10:13,466 --> 00:10:16,049
Nobody has to ask, "Where do I put this?"

180
00:10:16,783 --> 00:10:19,412
For QA engineers, it's about independence.

181
00:10:19,623 --> 00:10:23,977
They set up the scenario they need on
their own, like changing the date or

182
00:10:23,979 --> 00:10:28,636
sending a test push without asking a
developer and without a special build.

183
00:10:29,055 --> 00:10:33,003
For managers, it's a quick way to
check how the app behaves in different

184
00:10:33,004 --> 00:10:35,902
situations without asking anyone for help.

185
00:10:37,116 --> 00:10:41,181
We release every week, and
almost every release cycle needs

186
00:10:41,190 --> 00:10:43,164
some special setup of the app.

187
00:10:43,301 --> 00:10:47,730
So this is not a tool for rare
cases, it's a part of everyday work.

188
00:10:47,965 --> 00:10:52,226
And here is a platform lesson:
Your users aren't only engineers.

189
00:10:52,605 --> 00:10:57,556
When a tool lets other roles work on their
own, everyone saves time, including the

190
00:10:57,575 --> 00:11:04,653
engineers who get fewer interruptions Now
I wanna talk about the part that for me

191
00:11:04,742 --> 00:11:07,750
matters even more than the code, adoption.

192
00:11:08,064 --> 00:11:12,542
We never had a migration, never had
to convince anyone, and never had

193
00:11:12,544 --> 00:11:14,406
a rule saying you must use DevMode.

194
00:11:14,924 --> 00:11:15,956
People just use it.

195
00:11:16,191 --> 00:11:16,637
Why?

196
00:11:16,864 --> 00:11:18,149
I see three reasons.

197
00:11:19,812 --> 00:11:21,506
The first reason is timing.

198
00:11:21,763 --> 00:11:25,257
We build the framework before
internal tools had time to spread,

199
00:11:25,409 --> 00:11:27,077
so there was nothing to migrate.

200
00:11:27,130 --> 00:11:30,197
No old debug menus to move
and no owners to convince.

201
00:11:30,629 --> 00:11:33,622
The cheapest moment to
introduce a standard is before

202
00:11:33,659 --> 00:11:34,995
there is nothing to migrate.

203
00:11:35,372 --> 00:11:39,147
Later, the same standard costs
much more, not in code, but

204
00:11:39,173 --> 00:11:40,866
in meetings and discussions.

205
00:11:41,137 --> 00:11:42,818
And the price for us was small.

206
00:11:43,092 --> 00:11:44,816
I built the core in one spring.

207
00:11:46,302 --> 00:11:49,657
The second reason, the
easy way is the right way.

208
00:11:50,141 --> 00:11:53,416
Adding a plugin is simply faster
than building your own debug

209
00:11:53,419 --> 00:11:55,137
screen with your own navigation.

210
00:11:55,593 --> 00:11:58,366
So nobody had a reason to
go around the framework.

211
00:11:58,514 --> 00:12:01,122
Platform engineers call
this a golden path.

212
00:12:01,490 --> 00:12:03,212
You don't force people onto it.

213
00:12:03,475 --> 00:12:07,121
You make it the easiest option
and people choose it themselves.

214
00:12:08,265 --> 00:12:11,016
The third reason, ownership
follows the feature.

215
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:13,900
A new tool is usually
not a separate project.

216
00:12:14,161 --> 00:12:17,101
When the team discusses a
new business feature, we also

217
00:12:17,101 --> 00:12:18,781
think about how we'll test it.

218
00:12:19,273 --> 00:12:23,068
If we need a tool, the developer who
builds the feature also builds the

219
00:12:23,071 --> 00:12:25,240
plugin as a part of the same task.

220
00:12:26,102 --> 00:12:29,786
That person knows best which
data and states need to change.

221
00:12:30,194 --> 00:12:34,284
As a result, the core stays
small and stable, and the changes

222
00:12:34,307 --> 00:12:38,067
happen in the plugins where a
natural owner already exists.

223
00:12:38,540 --> 00:12:43,056
The platform itself needs
almost no maintenance Of course,

224
00:12:43,124 --> 00:12:44,525
not everything was perfect.

225
00:12:44,809 --> 00:12:47,978
If I started again today,
I would change two things.

226
00:12:48,408 --> 00:12:51,076
First, I would define
metrics from day one.

227
00:12:51,367 --> 00:12:55,207
For example, how long does it
take to prepare a test scenario

228
00:12:55,367 --> 00:12:56,999
with dev mode and without it?

229
00:12:57,458 --> 00:12:59,823
How often does QA need
help from a developer?

230
00:13:00,451 --> 00:13:04,141
Today, the value of dev mode is
obvious to the team, but I can show

231
00:13:04,142 --> 00:13:05,860
you a nice before and after chart.

232
00:13:06,589 --> 00:13:10,444
We prevented the problem instead of
fixing it, so there is no before.

233
00:13:11,018 --> 00:13:14,131
If you build something like
this, start measuring early.

234
00:13:14,524 --> 00:13:18,421
Even simple numbers help a lot
when you need to explain the value

235
00:13:18,471 --> 00:13:23,037
of your platform Second, I would
name a clear owner for the core.

236
00:13:23,300 --> 00:13:27,123
Right now, different developers improve
DevMod when they need something.

237
00:13:27,423 --> 00:13:29,946
That works while the
core is small and stable.

238
00:13:30,256 --> 00:13:33,298
But as the number of
plugins grows, risks appear.

239
00:13:33,860 --> 00:13:37,173
Plugin screens start to look
different, and old tools stay in the

240
00:13:37,174 --> 00:13:39,017
list long after anyone needs them.

241
00:13:39,631 --> 00:13:43,811
The owner and a few simple rules would
prevent that: who reviews changes to

242
00:13:43,814 --> 00:13:48,171
the core, how plugin screen should
look, and when we remove old tools.

243
00:13:50,104 --> 00:13:51,312
Is this for everyone?

244
00:13:51,579 --> 00:13:52,650
Not exactly.

245
00:13:53,386 --> 00:13:57,458
It fits best when your app has many
screens that depend on server data,

246
00:13:57,954 --> 00:14:01,977
when more than one role tests the
app, and when you release often.

247
00:14:02,511 --> 00:14:04,703
The bigger the app,
the more value you get.

248
00:14:05,068 --> 00:14:09,671
If your app is small or it's a short-lived
prototype, a full framework may be too

249
00:14:09,672 --> 00:14:12,675
much, and the simple debug screen is fine.

250
00:14:13,150 --> 00:14:17,892
But even then, I'd keep one rule: one
entry point for all internal tools.

251
00:14:18,542 --> 00:14:22,995
It costs almost nothing, and it makes it
easier to grow into a framework later.

252
00:14:23,627 --> 00:14:27,580
And if you have several apps, this
is where the approach pays off most.

253
00:14:28,097 --> 00:14:32,592
Each app brings its own plugins, and
all of them get the same shell for free.

254
00:14:33,681 --> 00:14:34,455
Let me sum up.

255
00:14:35,039 --> 00:14:36,944
Internal tools grow in every app.

256
00:14:37,271 --> 00:14:40,922
Without a system, they spread
everywhere, and everyone pays for

257
00:14:40,922 --> 00:14:43,521
it: developers, QA, and the company.

258
00:14:44,176 --> 00:14:46,914
Our answer was DevMode
as a plugin framework.

259
00:14:47,130 --> 00:14:50,649
The framework owns the shell,
activation, the main screen,

260
00:14:50,964 --> 00:14:52,813
the plugin list, and navigation.

261
00:14:53,302 --> 00:14:57,613
The app owns the content, the tools
themselves, and the two parts talk through

262
00:14:57,613 --> 00:15:03,146
a small contract Here are three lessons
you can take home whatever stack you use.

263
00:15:03,571 --> 00:15:07,413
One, build the shell early before
there's anything to migrate.

264
00:15:07,803 --> 00:15:10,326
Two, make the right way the easiest way.

265
00:15:10,668 --> 00:15:13,224
Then you don't need rules
to make people follow it.

266
00:15:13,492 --> 00:15:19,055
Three, design for all your users, not
only developers Thank you for listening.

267
00:15:19,222 --> 00:15:22,509
If you have any questions or
your own story about internal

268
00:15:22,546 --> 00:15:24,371
tools, find me on LinkedIn.

269
00:15:24,712 --> 00:15:25,941
I will be happy to talk
